{"narrative":"Since CP3/CP4, we ran a 6-week manual pilot (May-June 2024) in Tembisa using a WhatsApp Business number staffed by the founder as a stand-in for the eventual automated matching system. 4 tradespeople (2 electricians, 1 plumber, 1 appliance technician) piloted the service. Of these, 2 have converted to paying the 12% commission on completed jobs (the plumber and one electrician), tracked via a shared WhatsApp Business log of job requests, quotes, and confirmed completions, linked above. The other 2 tradespeople completed at least one matched job during the pilot but have not yet paid commission on a second job, so we count them as pilots, not paying clients, per our own conservative bar.

Paying clients (2): each has completed 3-4 paid jobs through the platform over 6 weeks and paid commission on all of them without prompting, which we treat as the strongest signal — commission collection worked via manual EFT/cash handoff tracked in the shared log, not yet an automated payment flow (that's a CP15/16 build item). Pilots (4 total, including the 2 paying): all four received at least one qualified lead through the WhatsApp number that they wouldn't have had otherwise, validating the core matching mechanism works operationally, even manually.

This is early and manual, and we are honest that automating the matching and payment collection (rather than the founder personally relaying WhatsApp messages) is the key unresolved execution risk — this pilot proves people-side demand and willingness to pay, not yet that the system works without the founder in the loop.","evidenceLink":"https://wa.me/business/quickfixtrades-pilot-log","b2b":{"pilots":4,"payingClients":2}}

